          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gamal Abdel Nasser's Early Life</strong><br>\nGamal Abdel Nasser was born in 1918 in Alexandria, Egypt. Growing up in an occupied Egypt, he developed a strong sense of nationalism. As a young man, Nasser was influenced by the anti-British resistance and the need for Arab unity. His early military career, which began in 1938 after his graduation from the Royal Military Academy, was marked by his participation in the 1948 Arab-Israeli War.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nNasser's rise to power began with his leadership of the Free Officers Movement, which aimed at overthrowing the monarchy and ending British dominance in Egypt. The movement's success led to the 1952 revolution, which resulted in the abdication of King Farouk and Nasser becoming the deputy prime minister in Egypt's new government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nasser as Prime Minister</strong><br>\nIn 1954, Nasser became Prime Minister of Egypt and started to implement social reforms and economic development projects. His policies focused on land redistribution and eradicating feudalism. Nasser's vision was to modernize Egypt through state-led industrialization and to gain political independence from Western powers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
