          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nationalization of the Suez Canal</strong><br>\nOne of Nasser's most defining acts was the nationalization of the Suez Canal in 1956. This move was aimed at financing the Aswan High Dam project after the withdrawal of U.S. and British financial support. The nationalization led to the Suez Crisis, where Egypt faced an invasion by Israel, followed by France and Britain, which ultimately increased Nasser's popularity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pan-Arabism and Arab Socialism</strong><br>\nNasser promoted Pan-Arabism and Arab Socialism, advocating for the unity of Arab countries and social justice. He became a symbol of Arab nationalism, challenging traditional monarchies in the region and seeking to establish a socialist order which would reduce the wealth gap within his nation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foreign Policy and Non-Alignment</strong><br>\nNasser played a central role in developing the non-aligned movement during the Cold War, refusing to take sides between the Soviet Union and the United States. His foreign policy was characterized by support for liberation movements and opposition to colonialism and imperialism, which gained him respect among developing nations.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
