          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The United Arab Republic</strong><br>\nIn 1958, Egypt and Syria formed the United Arab Republic, with Nasser as president, reflecting his commitment to Arab unity. However, the union was short-lived, as Syria withdrew in 1961. Despite this setback, Nasser continued to be an influential leader in the Arab world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Six-Day War</strong><br>\nThe Six-Day War in 1967 was a significant event during Nasser's presidency. Egypt's defeat by Israel led to the loss of the Sinai Peninsula. This loss tarnished Nasser's image as a champion of the Arab cause but did not diminish his commitment to the principles of Arab nationalism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nasser's Legacy</strong><br>\nNasser's death in 1970 marked the end of an era. He left behind a legacy that continues to influence Arab politics and society. His leadership is remembered for its ambitious social and economic reforms, efforts toward Arab unity, and a staunch stance against colonialism and imperialism. Nasser remains a controversial and significant figure in Middle Eastern history.</p>\n"
              },
